---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
---

<Base title="The pipeline on one page">
    <PageHeader num="02" title="The pipeline on one page" lede="One compile, six stages, each with a named artifact. This chapter is the map; the rest of the site is the territory." />

    <div class="prose prose-zinc max-w-none prose-headings:font-bold prose-a:text-teal-700 prose-code:before:content-none prose-code:after:content-none prose-code:rounded prose-code:bg-zinc-100 prose-code:px-1 prose-code:py-0.5 prose-code:text-[0.85em] prose-code:font-medium">
        <h2>What compiles, what runs</h2>
        <p>
            Everything interesting in this engine happens inside <code>Pattern.compile</code>. That's where a pattern string becomes a small program — a DFA plus register operations — and it's the part this site spends most of its time on. Match time, by contrast, is deliberately simple: a walk over flat integer arrays (or generated bytecode) that consumes one character per step.
        </p>
        <p>The compile pipeline, with the artifacts each stage hands to the next:</p>
    </div>

    <div class="not-prose my-6 space-y-2">
        {[
            { n: '1', stage: 'Parser', cls: 'parser/Parser.java', in: 'pattern text', out: 'ParseResult: AST + tagCount + groupCount + flags', body: 'Iterative-descent parse. A capturing group is immediately rewritten to tag · body · tag, so the tree carries no "group" node — just integers marking boundaries. Nesting depth costs heap (budgeted), not JVM stack.' },
            { n: '2', stage: 'FixedTags', cls: 'ast/FixedTags.java', in: 'AST', out: 'annotations: fixedOn / fixedOffset per tag', body: 'Marks tags whose value can be recomputed arithmetically at match end (the close of (abc) is just open+3). Those tags are then omitted from everything downstream — no transitions, no registers.' },
            { n: '3', stage: 'Tnfa.Builder', cls: 'tnfa/Tnfa.java', in: 'annotated AST', out: 'Tnfa: flat int arrays of states, symbol edges, and epsilon edges carrying (priority, tag, assertion mask)', body: 'Thompson construction with tagged, prioritized edges. Priorities encode leftmost-first preference; negative tags encode "group did not participate" structurally; bounded repeats are desugared here.' },
            { n: '4', stage: 'TdfaCompiler', cls: 'tdfa/TdfaCompiler.java', in: 'Tnfa', out: 'DeterminizedDfa: DFA states (kernels), transition/register ops, accept tables', body: 'Subset construction. Each DFA state is a priority-ordered list of TNFA configurations; tag histories are resolved at compile time into SET/COPY ops on transitions. Runs the worklist, then derives per-state accept/stop tables and final ops.' },
            { n: '5', stage: 'TdfaMaterializer', cls: 'tdfa/TdfaMaterializer.java', in: 'DeterminizedDfa', out: 'Tdfa: the frozen artifact — flat arrays', body: 'Runs regopt (a liveness + interference register allocator over the ops), flattens builders into int[] tables, minimizes (register-aware Moore), sorts ranges, picks table tiers.' },
            { n: '6', stage: 'Engine translation', cls: 'asm/TdfaAsmBackend.java or tdfa/TdfaRunner.java', in: 'Tdfa', out: 'a RegexEngine: generated JVM class, or the interpreter', out2: true, body: 'The default backend emits one class per pattern with the walk inlined as bytecode. -Dtdfa.engine=VM skips codegen and interprets the flat arrays directly — zero dependencies, Java 8 floor.' },
        ].map((s) => (
            <div class="flex gap-4 rounded-lg border border-zinc-200 bg-white p-4">
                <div class="flex h-7 w-7 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-teal-700 font-mono text-xs font-bold text-white">{s.n}</div>
                <div class="min-w-0">
                    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2">
                        <span class="font-semibold text-zinc-900">{s.stage}</span>
                        <span class="mono text-xs text-zinc-400">{s.cls}</span>
                    </div>
                    <p class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-600">{s.body}</p>
                    <div class="mono mt-2 text-[11px] text-zinc-400">{s.in} <span class="text-teal-600">→</span> <span class="text-teal-700">{s.out}</span></div>
                </div>
            </div>
        ))}
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-zinc max-w-none prose-headings:font-bold prose-a:text-teal-700 prose-code:before:content-none prose-code:after:content-none prose-code:rounded prose-code:bg-zinc-100 prose-code:px-1 prose-code:py-0.5 prose-code:text-[0.85em] prose-code:font-medium">
        <h2>Watching it happen</h2>
        <p>
            Compilation reports its progress through a <code>CompileObserver</code>; the stages fire in this order (the enum's declaration order is historical — regopt actually runs before minimize):
        </p>
    </div>

    <Code lang="text" title="observer stages, in execution order">{`PARSE → TNFA → DETERMINIZE → REGOPT → MINIMIZE → ENGINE
          \\_ Tnfa.compile _/        \\__ TdfaMaterializer.run __/   \\_ backend _/
                                     (inside Tdfa.compile)`}</Code>

    <div class="prose prose-zinc max-w-none prose-headings:font-bold prose-a:text-teal-700 prose-code:before:content-none prose-code:after:content-none prose-code:rounded prose-code:bg-zinc-100 prose-code:px-1 prose-code:py-0.5 prose-code:text-[0.85em] prose-code:font-medium">
        <p>
            The seam between stage 4 and 5 is a deliberate lifetime boundary: <code>TdfaCompiler</code> holds gigabytes-scale scratch for big patterns (kernels, an interning index, closure buffers), and its instance becomes unreachable the moment <code>compile()</code> returns — the phase boundary <em>is</em> the memory-lifetime boundary. <code>DeterminizedDfa</code> is the frozen value that crosses it.
        </p>

        <h2>The three ideas that make it work</h2>
        <p>Three ideas carry the design; every chapter is one of them in detail:</p>
        <ol class="list-decimal pl-6">
            <li>
                <strong>Tags on epsilon transitions</strong> (<a href="/tnfa/">TNFA</a>). Instead of "capturing is what the interpreter writes down," capture boundaries become marks on graph edges, and <em>preference</em> between alternatives becomes an integer priority on those same edges. The automaton itself now knows which match a backtracker would have chosen.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Compile-time resolution of tag histories</strong> (<a href="/determinization/">determinization</a>). Subset construction tracks, per DFA-state configuration, the small suffix of "tags set since the last character." The instant a character is consumed, that suffix is converted into concrete register writes on the transition — and forgotten. Unbounded history, bounded bookkeeping, finite automaton.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Registers as a compile target</strong> (<a href="/registers/">registers</a>, <a href="/asm/">bytecode</a>). What's left after determinization is a DFA plus a straight-line mini-program of register ops per transition. That shape is low-level enough for a classic register allocator to optimize — and low-level enough to compile to JVM bytecode as <code>regs[i] = pos</code> stores.
            </li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Modules and artifacts</h2>
        <p>
            The repo is a Gradle multi-project build with three shipping artifacts:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6">
            <li><code>tdfa</code> — the facade: the re2j-shaped <code>Pattern</code>/<code>Matcher</code> API you import.</li>
            <li><code>tdfa-asm</code> — the default backend; per-pattern bytecode generation via ASM.</li>
            <li><code>tdfa-core</code> — everything, interpreter-only, zero dependencies, Java 8 floor. Run with <code>-Dtdfa.engine=VM</code> and no code is ever generated.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            One subtlety worth flagging now: <code>find()</code> and <code>matches()</code> share ONE automaton. <code>find()</code> uses the pruned artifact (leftmost-first semantics baked in with a "stop" table); <code>matches()</code> — whole-input match with captures — walks the same tables, except at the spots where the pruning would have discarded captures a full match needs (<code>(a|ab)</code> against <code>"ab"</code>), where a <em>side table</em> recorded during the same determinization holds the un-pruned continuations. When that bounded side exploration runs over budget, the compile fails with the standard "pattern too large" family — unless you declare up front that you never call whole-input methods: <code>Pattern.compileFind(...)</code> / the <code>FIND_ONLY</code> flag skip the whole machinery entirely (whole-input methods then throw <code>UnsupportedOperationException</code>). More on that in <a href="/execution/">Execution</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Reading order</h2>
        <p>
            Straight through is the intended path — each chapter assumes the previous. If you only care about one thing: the determinization chapter is the algorithmic heart, and its playground is the one to play with. If you're evaluating the library for use, read <a href="/budgets/">Budgets</a> and <a href="/testing/">Testing</a>.
        </p>
    </div>
</Base>
